If a jet can create a sonic boom breaking the sound barrier, what sound does a magnetic wave make when it breaks the sound barrier
Apr 20

Magnetic waves (electromagnetic waves) don't make any sound when they "break the sound barrier" — and here's why that concept doesn't quite apply:

A sonic boom requires a medium. Sound is a pressure wave traveling through matter (air, water, etc.). When a jet exceeds the speed of sound in air, it outruns the pressure waves it creates, causing them to pile up into a shockwave — that's the boom you hear.

Electromagnetic waves are fundamentally different. They don't compress a medium; they oscillate electric and magnetic fields and can travel through a complete vacuum. There's nothing to "pile up," so no shockwave, no boom.

The relevant speed limit for EM waves is the speed of light, not sound. Nothing with mass can reach it, and the consequences of approaching it are relativistic (time dilation, length contraction) — not acoustic.

The closest real analogy is actually called Cherenkov radiation — the electromagnetic equivalent of a sonic boom. When a charged particle (like an electron) travels through a medium faster than light travels through that same medium (light slows down in glass or water, even though nothing exceeds c in a vacuum), it produces a blue glow. It's visually striking but completely silent, since it's a light phenomenon, not a sound one.

So the short answer: no sound at all — but you do get an eerie blue glow.

