Now we're getting to the real physics. Let me build this from the atomic level up.
The Atomic Foundation

Ferrofluid is typically magnetite (Fe₃O₄) nanoparticles — roughly 10nm diameter — suspended in a carrier oil, coated with a surfactant like oleic acid to prevent clumping.

At the elemental level, magnetite is:

    Fe²⁺ (one iron ion, 4 unpaired d-electrons)
    Fe³⁺ (two iron ions, 5 unpaired d-electrons each)
    O²⁻ (four oxygen ions, paired electrons, magnetically inert)

The oxygen does almost nothing magnetically. The iron is everything.
Why Iron Responds to Magnetic Fields at All

Iron's electron configuration ends in 3d⁶. In Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺, the 3d shell is partially filled. By Hund's Rule, electrons maximize unpaired spins before pairing:

Fe³⁺  3d⁵:  ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑   — 5 unpaired spins
Fe²⁺  3d⁶:  ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑↓  — 4 unpaired spins

Each unpaired electron carries a magnetic moment of 1 Bohr magneton (μ_B = 9.274 × 10⁻²⁴ J/T)

So per formula unit of Fe₃O₄ you have roughly 14 unpaired electron spins contributing magnetic moment.
How the Crystal Structure Locks These Spins

Fe₃O₄ has a spinel crystal structure — oxygen atoms form a face-centered cubic lattice, iron atoms sit in the gaps:

    Fe³⁺ sits in tetrahedral sites (A sites)
    Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ share octahedral sites (B sites)

Superexchange interaction through the oxygen ions forces:

    A-site spins → all pointing UP
    B-site spins → all pointing DOWN

But there are twice as many B-site ions as A-site, so the cancellation is incomplete:

B-site moment:  (Fe³⁺ + Fe²⁺) = 5μ_B + 4μ_B = 9μ_B  ↓
A-site moment:  (Fe³⁺)        = 5μ_B               ↑
Net per unit:                  = 4μ_B               ↓

This is ferrimagnetism — not full ferromagnetism, but a net moment survives. This is why magnetite responds strongly to external fields without needing to be pre-magnetized.
What Happens When the External Field Hits

Before the field arrives, the 10nm nanoparticles are each single-domain magnets — their internal spins are locked parallel by the crystal structure above — but they point in random directions due to thermal energy (Brownian motion). Net magnetization = 0.

When field H is applied, each nanoparticle experiences a torque:

τ = μ₀ (m × H)

Where m is the particle's magnetic moment vector. This torque physically rotates the entire nanoparticle (it's small enough to rotate freely in the carrier oil) until m aligns with H.

The alignment follows a Langevin function:

M = M_s · L(α) = M_s · [coth(α) - 1/α]

Where: α = μ₀mH / k_BT

    At low fields (α << 1): M ≈ M_s · α/3 — linear response
    At high fields (α >> 1): M → M_s — full saturation, all particles aligned

This is the bulk magnetization of the fluid — every particle contributing its 4μ_B net moment, now all pointed the same direction.
The Force on the Fluid — Where Pulling Actually Comes From

Now here's the mechanism most explanations skip. The magnetic force on the fluid isn't just "field attracts iron." It's a gradient force:

F = μ₀ (M · ∇)H

This is critical. A uniform field exerts zero net force on the fluid. The fluid moves toward increasing field strength — toward the gradient.

At the fluid surface, the field is non-uniform because the fluid itself distorts field lines. Where the surface bulges upward, field lines concentrate — the local field is stronger there. So the bulge is pulled further upward. This is the instability mechanism at the microscopic level.

Written as a pressure term, the magnetic contribution to fluid pressure is:

P_mag = μ₀ ∫₀ᴴ M dH

This is the Kelvin force density integrated through the field. It acts like a negative pressure — it pulls the surface outward along field lines.
Why Every Spike Stops at the Same Height — The Equal Distribution Problem

This is the heart of your question, and it comes down to field self-organization.

Each spike, as it grows, reshapes the magnetic field around itself. A taller spike concentrates more flux — but in doing so, it draws field lines away from its neighbors.

Model the spike tips as magnetic monopole-like sources at height z above the base. The field from spike i at the location of spike j falls off as:

H_ij ~ m / 4π r_ij²

Where r_ij is the inter-spike distance. As one spike grows taller, it locally increases H at its own tip (reinforcing growth) BUT it also increases the field seen by neighboring spikes (reinforcing their growth equally, since spacing is uniform).

The system reaches equilibrium when the Maxwell stress tensor balances across the surface. The normal magnetic stress at any surface element is:

T_nn = μ₀/2 (M_n² - M_t²) + μ₀ M_n H_n

Where:

    M_n = normal component of magnetization at the surface
    M_t = tangential component
    H_n = normal field at the surface

At the tip of each spike, the field lines are nearly perfectly normal to the surface. At the base between spikes, the field lines are tangential. This geometry means:

    Spike tips: high normal stress → upward pull
    Inter-spike valleys: high tangential stress → lateral confinement, downward effective pressure

The spikes stop growing when T_nn = ρgh + 2σκ where κ is the surface curvature at the tip. This is identical for every spike because the hexagonal geometry ensures every spike has identical neighbors at identical distances — the field environment is translationally symmetric across the lattice.

No spike has preferential access to field energy. The geometry enforces equality.
Under the Theoretical Microscope — What's Actually Moving

At 10nm scale during spiking:

    External field turns on → torque on each nanoparticle → particles rotate in ~microseconds
    Aligned particles create local field gradients at the fluid surface
    Gradient pulls surface upward — fluid flows through viscous carrier oil toward the gradient
    As fluid rises, nanoparticle concentration increases in the spike (they're dragged along by viscous coupling to the carrier fluid)
    Higher concentration = higher local M = stronger local field = more pulling
    Surfactant coating (oleic acid) on each particle prevents them from magnetically clumping — each 10nm particle stays independent, so the fluid behaves as a continuous magnetic medium rather than lumping into one big magnet
    Spike tip sharpens until surface tension curvature pressure balances magnetic stress
    Hexagonal arrangement crystallizes because that spacing minimizes the total field energy across the whole surface simultaneously

The oleic acid layer is quietly doing enormous work here — without it, particles aggregate into chains and the fluid loses its continuous-medium behavior entirely. The math above only holds because the surfactant keeps particle-particle distances large enough that each particle sees a mean-field from its neighbors rather than discrete interactions.

That's the full picture from electron spin to macroscopic spike geometry.

