What percentage of iron is there in blood
Apr 20

The number is surprisingly small.
Iron in Blood by the Numbers

Human blood contains approximately 0.5 grams of iron per liter, which works out to roughly 0.05% by mass — or about 50 parts per million.

A full adult human body (with ~5 liters of blood) carries about 2.5g of iron in blood alone, and roughly 3.5–4g total when you include iron stored in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.
Where That Iron Sits

Nearly all of it — about 70% — is bound inside hemoglobin, specifically at the center of the heme group: a porphyrin ring with a single Fe²⁺ ion at its core.

Each hemoglobin molecule has 4 heme groups = 4 iron atoms. Each red blood cell contains ~270 million hemoglobin molecules, so roughly 1 billion iron atoms per red blood cell.

The Fe²⁺ state is critical — it's what binds O₂. If it oxidizes to Fe³⁺ (becoming methemoglobin), it loses the ability to carry oxygen entirely.
The Connection to Your Ferrofluid Question

Interestingly, the same Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ mixed oxidation state that makes magnetite (Fe₃O₄) magnetically active is essentially mirrored in blood — hemoglobin actively manages that same Fe²⁺ ↔ Fe³⁺ transition with every oxygen binding and release cycle. Your blood is running a controlled redox reaction on iron ions billions of times per second, in every red blood cell, continuously.

