Now you're into genuinely deep and underexplored territory. Let's build this carefully from first principles.
The Baseline — Blood is Already an Electrolyte Circuit

Blood isn't just iron in water. It's a conducting electrolyte solution containing:

    Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ — positive charge carriers
    Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻ — negative charge carriers
    Plasma conductivity ≈ 0.7 S/m
    Whole blood conductivity ≈ 0.3 S/m (red blood cells reduce conductivity)

So before any external EMF is applied, blood already behaves as a volume conductor — charges move freely through it in response to any electric potential difference.
What EMF Does to Red Blood Cells Specifically

Red blood cells (RBCs) are biconcave discs ~8μm diameter with a lipid bilayer membrane that acts as a capacitor:

C_membrane ≈ 0.9 μF/cm²

The resting membrane potential is approximately -10mV (much lower than neurons, ~-70mV).

When an external EMF is applied, the cell membrane charges like a capacitor. The transmembrane voltage induced is:

V_m = 1.5 · E · r · cos(θ) · [1 - exp(-t/τ)]

Where:

    E = external electric field strength
    r = cell radius (~4μm)
    θ = angle between field and membrane normal
    τ = membrane charging time constant ≈ 0.3 μs

The 1.5 factor comes from the spherical geometry — the cell focuses the external field, concentrating it across the membrane beyond the ambient field strength.
Cell Polarization and What It Actually Means Molecularly

When the transmembrane voltage exceeds a critical threshold — roughly 200-300mV — something dramatic happens:

Electroporation — the lipid bilayer locally ruptures, forming transient pores ~1-20nm in diameter.

But before that extreme, at lower EMF, subtler polarization effects occur:

The Fe²⁺ in hemoglobin inside the cell is now sitting in a polarized electric environment. The heme group's porphyrin ring is a conjugated aromatic system — it has delocalized π electrons that respond to electric fields by redistributing charge asymmetrically across the ring.

This does two things:

    Shifts the Fe²⁺ electron density — slightly altering its oxidation state equilibrium toward Fe³⁺ depending on field direction
    Changes O₂ binding affinity — the Bohr effect already shows that pH and CO₂ shift hemoglobin's oxygen curve; EMF-induced charge redistribution creates an analogous but electromagnetic Bohr-like shift

The Current Generation — Faraday at the Cellular Level

Now the interesting part. A polarized RBC in a magnetic field becomes a current source through two mechanisms:

Mechanism 1 — Ionic redistribution: The polarized membrane drives Na⁺ and K⁺ to redistribute around the cell surface. Moving ions = current. Each cell generates a tiny current dipole:

p = 4π · σ_i · r³ · E / (σ_i + 2σ_e)

Where σ_i = intracellular conductivity, σ_e = extracellular conductivity.

Mechanism 2 — The iron itself under combined E and B fields: Here's where it gets remarkable. The Fe²⁺ ions inside hemoglobin, now carrying drift velocity from the electric field, experience a Lorentz force from the magnetic field:

F = q(E + v × B)

The v × B term deflects iron ions perpendicular to both their drift direction and the magnetic field. In a blood vessel this creates a Hall effect in biological tissue — charge separation across the vessel diameter, generating a transverse voltage:

V_Hall = (I · B) / (n · q · d)

Where:

    I = current along vessel
    B = magnetic field strength
    n = charge carrier density
    d = vessel diameter

This has actually been measured in aortas under MRI magnetic fields — the MHD (magnetohydrodynamic) effect in blood is real and produces millivolt-scale voltages in major vessels at clinical MRI field strengths (1.5-3T).
The Magnetic Field Generated by Polarized Cells

Now the cells are generating current. Current generates magnetic field. For a single blood vessel modeled as a current-carrying cylinder, the field outside it is:

B_vessel = μ₀I / 2πr

At the scale of capillaries (~5μm diameter) carrying picoamp currents this is negligible. But:

Coherent polarization across large tissue volumes changes the calculation entirely. If an external EMF polarizes millions of cells simultaneously in the same orientation, their individual magnetic dipole moments add constructively:

B_total = μ₀ · N · m_cell / 4πr³

Where N = number of coherently polarized cells. This is analogous to how ferrofluid nanoparticles go from random (no net field) to aligned (strong net field) — except here the "particles" are entire red blood cells.
The Feedback Loop Nobody Talks About

This is where it becomes a genuinely complex dynamical system:

    External EMF polarizes RBC membranes
    Polarized cells redistribute ions → generate micro-currents
    Micro-currents in blood vessel under ambient B field → Hall voltage
    Hall voltage adds to or subtracts from the original EMF locally
    Modified local EMF re-polarizes neighboring cells differently
    Iron oxidation state (Fe²⁺ ↔ Fe³⁺) shifts slightly under sustained polarization
    Shifted oxidation state changes local magnetic susceptibility of the blood
    Changed susceptibility alters how the external field couples into the tissue

The blood is now acting as a nonlinear magnetically active medium — not unlike ferrofluid, but with active ionic and biochemical feedback layered on top of the passive magnetic response.
At Massive EMF — The Breakdown Regime

At truly high field strengths the picture becomes severe:

    >200mV transmembrane: electroporation, pore formation, cell contents leak
    Sustained high field: Fe²⁺ oxidizes irreversibly to Fe³⁺ → methemoglobinemia at the cellular level — cells lose oxygen carrying capacity
    MHD forces on blood flow: at high enough B fields the Lorentz force on flowing blood measurably opposes flow — the heart works harder against a magnetic brake
    Radical generation: Fe²⁺ under oxidative stress participates in Fenton reactions: Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Fe³⁺ + OH• + OH⁻ — generating hydroxyl radicals that attack lipids and DNA

The same iron chemistry that makes ferrofluid spike so elegantly becomes deeply destructive when driven far from equilibrium in a biological context.

The system is the same physics — polarization, alignment, field-matter coupling — but now embedded in a self-regulating living system that has extremely narrow tolerances for deviation.

