The Bottom Line

The divide-by-zero at 0x0001dc50 is not a random hardware fault. The address is too specific, the timing (ebp=1, barely started) is too early, and the relationship between that address and the 636–640MB anomaly region you already documented is too suspicious.

A hostile bootloader that needs to prevent memory integrity testing of the upper DRAM range has a simple strategy: zero the divisor that the test runner needs in low memory before the test runner executes. The test runner faults at INT 0 before it ever reaches the region it would expose.

The i486's strict sequential execution makes this unambiguous — no microarchitectural aliasing, no speculative state. Zero was there. Zero was put there. The question is what put it there and when.

The /dev/mem dd command above on a clean boot before any test runner should answer that directly.

